---
source: crates/perasc/src/tests.rs
expression: err
---
error[E0006]: `===` does not exist in Peras
  --> tests/fixtures/demo.peras:3:17
   |
 3 |     const zero = b === 0.0;
   |                    ^^^ write `==`
   |
   = why: Peras never converts types implicitly, so `==` and `!=` already compare strictly, like `===` and `!==` in JavaScript.
   = fix: replace `===` with `==`
   |
 3 -     const zero = b === 0.0;
 3 +     const zero = b == 0.0;
   |
   = details: perasc explain E0006

error[E0002]: unterminated string
  --> tests/fixtures/demo.peras:4:15
   |
 4 |   let label = "ratio<U+2067>;
   |               ^ this string is never closed
   |                              - the line ends here, before the closing `"`
   |
   = why: A string must close on the line where it starts; to put a line break in it, write `\n`.
   = fix: close the string with `"`
   |
 4 -   let label = "ratio<U+2067>;
 4 +   let label = "ratio<U+2067>";
   |
   = details: perasc explain E0002

error[E0007]: invisible bidirectional control character U+2067 (RIGHT-TO-LEFT ISOLATE)
  --> tests/fixtures/demo.peras:4:21
   |
 4 |   let label = "ratio<U+2067>;
   |                     ^^^^^^^^ this invisible character changes the order in which the code is displayed
   |
   = why: A bidirectional control character can make code look different from what it compiles to (the Trojan Source attack, CVE-2021-42574), so it is refused everywhere, strings and comments included.
   = fix: write it as the escape `\u{2067}`, which stays visible
   |
 4 -   let label = "ratio<U+2067>;
 4 +   let label = "ratio\u{2067};
   |
   = details: perasc explain E0007

error[E0001]: invalid character − (U+2212)
  --> tests/fixtures/demo.peras:5:12
   |
 5 |   return a − b * 10px;
   |            ^ this character is not allowed in Peras code
   |
   = why: Outside strings and comments, Peras code only uses ASCII letters, digits, `_`, spaces and the operators of the language.
   = fix: replace − with `-`, the ASCII character it looks like
   |
 5 -   return a − b * 10px;
 5 +   return a - b * 10px;
   |
   = details: perasc explain E0001

error[E0005]: malformed number `10px`
  --> tests/fixtures/demo.peras:5:20
   |
 5 |   return a − b * 10px;
   |                    ^^ unexpected `px` after the number
   |
   = why: Number literals have no unit or type suffix: the type comes from the context or from an annotation.
   = fix: remove `px`; to choose a type, annotate the variable, e.g. `let x: f32 = 5.0`
   |
 5 -   return a − b * 10px;
 5 +   return a − b * 10;
   |
   = details: perasc explain E0005

